{"name":"Akuma","hex":"#d63136","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"akuma","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Japanese demon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/akuma"}],"entries":[{"title":"Seikima-II","blocks":[{"text":"Seikima-II  is a Japanese heavy metal band, formed in 1982. Throughout their career they have had numerous lineups, with lead singer Demon Kakka the sole constant member. The group has sold over 10 million records in Japan alone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Japan_Expo_2010_-_Concert_Seikima-II_-_P1470363.jpg/330px-Japan_Expo_2010_-_Concert_Seikima-II_-_P1470363.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seikima-II"},{"title":"Karateka (video game)","blocks":[{"text":"Karateka is a 1984 martial arts action game written for the Apple II by Jordan Mechner. It is his first published game and was created while he was attending Yale University. The game was published in North America by Broderbund and in Europe by Ariolasoft. Along with Karate Champ and Yie Ar Kung-Fu, Karateka is one of the earliest martial arts fighting games. It was inspired by Japanese culture and by early Disney animated films and silent pictures. An influential game of its era, it was one of the first to use cinematic storytelling and sound design, and rotoscoped animation."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/ef/Karateka_Coverart.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karateka_(video_game)"},{"title":"Akuma (Street Fighter)","blocks":[{"text":"Akuma , known in Japan as Gouki , is a fictional character from the Street Fighter series of fighting games created by Capcom. Akuma made his debut in Super Street Fighter II Turbo as a secret character and an alternative boss to the villain M. Bison. In the storyline, he is the younger brother of Gouken, Ryu's and Ken's master. After defeating his brother, Akuma gains interest in several fighters, most notably Ryu as he senses that the protagonist has a similar power to him known as the Satsui no Hadou. In some games, he also has an alternate version named Shin Akuma or Shin Gouki  in Japanese and Oni Akuma in Super Street Fighter IV: Arcade Edition."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f0/Akuma_%28Street_Fighter%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akuma_(Street_Fighter)"},{"title":"Akuma (folklore)","blocks":[{"text":"An akuma  is an evil spirit in Japanese folklore, sometimes described in English-language sources as a devil or demon. An alternative name for the akuma is ma (ま). Akuma is the name assigned to Satan in Japanese Christianity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Amaterasu_cave_wide.jpg/330px-Amaterasu_cave_wide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akuma_(folklore)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_50.jpg/500px-Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_50.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_50.jpg/1280px-Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_50.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_50.jpg/500px-Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_50.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Demon Kakka and Jail O'Hashi performing in 2010.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASeikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_50.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Japan_Expo_2010_-_Concert_Seikima-II_-_P1470415.jpg/500px-Japan_Expo_2010_-_Concert_Seikima-II_-_P1470415.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Japan_Expo_2010_-_Concert_Seikima-II_-_P1470415.jpg/1280px-Japan_Expo_2010_-_Concert_Seikima-II_-_P1470415.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Japan_Expo_2010_-_Concert_Seikima-II_-_P1470415.jpg/500px-Japan_Expo_2010_-_Concert_Seikima-II_-_P1470415.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Demon Kakka performing the song \"Dead Symphony\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJapan_Expo_2010_-_Concert_Seikima-II_-_P1470415.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_09.jpg/120px-Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_09.jpg/250px-Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_09.jpg/120px-Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASeikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_09.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_72.jpg/120px-Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_72.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_72.jpg/250px-Seikima-II_20100704_Japan_Expo_72.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thum